course: biology
lesson: 116
title: Famous zoos in the USA and the UK
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The short word «zoo» comes from London: London Zoo opened in 1828 as the Zoological Gardens, a zoo for science, and people shortened the long name to «zoo».
explain: London Zoo built the first public aquarium and an early reptile house, and a black bear there called Winnipeg gave her name to a boy's teddy and so to Winnie-the-Pooh.
explain: In America, San Diego Zoo grew out of animals left over from a big exhibition, the Bronx Zoo sent bison back to the Great Plains, and the free National Zoo in Washington became famous for its giant pandas.
explain: Britain has Chester Zoo, its most visited, Edinburgh Zoo with its penguins, the huge Whipsnade, and safari parks where you drive past lions with the windows shut; every one of them needs a licence by law.
fact: San Diego Zoo in California started in 1916 with animals left over from an exhibition; it runs big conservation research, including the Frozen Zoo and condor breeding, and has a safari park nearby.
fact: The Bronx Zoo in New York opened in 1899 and is run by the Wildlife Conservation Society, which also funds and runs conservation work in many countries; in 1907 it sent bison to the Great Plains to help save the American bison.
fact: The Smithsonian's National Zoo in Washington is free and is famous for giant pandas sent by China since 1972; its pandas went back to China in 2023 and new pandas arrived in 2024.
fact: London Zoo opened in 1828 as a zoo for science, set up by the Zoological Society of London, which still does research and conservation worldwide.
fact: A black bear at London Zoo called Winnipeg inspired the name of Winnie-the-Pooh.
fact: London Zoo opened the world's first public aquarium in 1853 and an early reptile house in 1849, and the word «zoo» comes from its early name, the Zoological Gardens.
fact: Chester Zoo is the most visited zoo in the UK; it was founded by George Mottershead, whose story was told in the TV series «Our Zoo», and it leads many breeding programmes.
fact: Edinburgh Zoo is famous for its penguins and had giant pandas; Whipsnade, near London, covers about 240 hectares and is one of the largest zoos in Europe.
fact: In safari parks animals roam large areas while people drive through with the windows closed, and at some British safari parks monkeys climb on cars and pull off rubber parts.
fact: The Georgia Aquarium in Atlanta is one of the largest aquariums in the world; its main tank holds millions of litres and keeps whale sharks and manta rays, though critics say even it is too small for whale sharks, which swim across whole oceans.
fact: The USA has over 200 accredited zoos and aquariums with well over a hundred million visits a year, and they share breeding plans and fund conservation together.
fact: In the UK every zoo needs a licence and regular inspections under the Zoo Licensing Act of 1981, and some zoos have been closed after failing them.
exercise: When did San Diego Zoo start, and with what? | 1916, with animals left over from an exhibition | 1828, with lions and bears given by the English king | 1752, with horses and cows from a nearby farm | 2016, with animals from a closed circus
exercise: What does San Diego Zoo keep in its Frozen Zoo? | Frozen cells of rare animals | Blocks of ice for the polar bears to lick | Frozen fish for the penguins | Statues of extinct animals
exercise: Who runs the Bronx Zoo? | The Wildlife Conservation Society | The Smithsonian museums in Washington | The Zoological Society of London | The city's fire service
exercise: How did the Bronx Zoo help the American bison in 1907? | It sent bison to the Great Plains | It cloned the last bison | It bought all bison for its own park | It moved bison to London
exercise: What is special about the National Zoo in Washington? | It is free and famous for its giant pandas | It is the oldest zoo in the whole world | It only keeps animals that come from America | It is open only at night
exercise: When did London Zoo open, and why? | 1828, as a zoo for science | 1066, as a castle zoo | 1953, as a film studio | 1899, as a city farm
exercise: Which famous book character's name came from a bear at London Zoo? | Winnie-the-Pooh | Paddington | Baloo | Yogi Bear
exercise: What did London Zoo open in 1853, the first of its kind for the public? | An aquarium | A cinema | A cable car | A dinosaur park
exercise: Where does the word «zoo» come from? | A short form of London's Zoological Gardens | The roaring sound a lion makes at feeding time | The name of the first zoo keeper | A Latin word for cage
exercise: Which is the most visited zoo in the UK? | Chester Zoo | Whipsnade Zoo | Edinburgh Zoo | Bristol Zoo
exercise: Who founded Chester Zoo? | George Mottershead | Steve Irwin | Queen Victoria | Winnie the bear
exercise: What is Edinburgh Zoo famous for? | Its penguins, and for having had giant pandas | Its orcas and dolphins in a huge sea pool | Its real dinosaur skeletons from Scotland | Being the biggest zoo in Europe
exercise: Why do big zoos like Whipsnade often lie outside cities? | There is room to give big animals more space | City people dislike animals and do not want them near | Animals are banned from cities | Land in the countryside is always free to use
exercise: Why must car windows stay closed in a safari park? | The animals roam freely and could be dangerous | The animals do not like the smell of the air from cars | It keeps the cars clean | The animals are behind thick glass walls anyway
exercise: What swims in the main tank of the Georgia Aquarium? | Whale sharks and manta rays | Killer whales and polar bears | Crocodiles and hippos | Penguins and seals
exercise: Which law makes every UK zoo need a licence? | The Zoo Licensing Act of 1981 | The Animal Freedom Act of 1066 | The Zoo Party Law of 2016 | There is no law about zoos
exercise: How many accredited zoos and aquariums does the USA have? | Over 200 | About 5 | About 20,000 | Exactly 50, one per state
test: «London Zoo is the oldest zoo in the world» What is right? | It is the oldest scientific zoo; Vienna's Schönbrunn is older | Yes, it opened before any other zoo anywhere on Earth | No, it opened in 1990 | No, London has never had a zoo, only farms and parks for animals
test: «monkeys nicked the wiper off our car at the safari park, mad» Why do safari park monkeys do this? | They are curious and pull at rubber parts of cars | They are trained by the keepers to steal from visitors | They want to drive the car | They think the rubber wiper is a tasty bit of food
test: Disney's Winnie the Pooh is a honey-loving talking bear. What was the real Winnipeg? | A black bear at London Zoo whose name inspired the stories | A grizzly that lived in a hollow tree in the Hundred Acre Wood | A teddy bear made in America | A brown bear at a Canadian farm that really ate only honey
test: What would happen to a zoo in Britain that failed its inspections again and again? | It could be closed | Nothing, inspections are just advice | It would be given more animals | It would become a museum by law
test: «whale shark in a tank, sounds sick» What do critics say? | Even a huge tank may be too small for an animal that swims across oceans | Whale sharks prefer small tanks | Whale sharks are only a few centimetres long, so any tank is far too big for them | Whale sharks can live without water for days, so they do not need a tank at all
test: How can a city zoo like the Bronx Zoo help animals far away? | Its society funds and runs conservation work in many countries | It flies every one of its animals back home on holiday each year | It sends its keepers on holiday | It cannot help animals outside the city, so it does not try
test: In the film Night at the Museum the animals come alive at night. What is the real National Zoo famous for? | Giant pandas sent by China since 1972 | Real dinosaur skeletons that walk at night | Talking monkeys | A lion that guards the White House garden
test: Why do so many people visit zoos? | To see real animals up close, learn and have a day out | Zoo tickets are free everywhere | People must visit a zoo once a year by law, or pay a fine | There is nothing else at all to do in cities at the weekend
test: «5 penguins at Edinburgh waddled past, and erm 2 more, how many feet» | 14, two feet for each of 7 penguins | 10, two feet for each of the 5 penguins | 28, four feet for each of the 7 penguins | 7, one foot for each penguin
test: «we drove past 3 lions and 2 rhinos, how many tails then» | 5, one tail each | 10, two tails each | 3, rhinos have no tails | 20, four tails each
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the origin of the word «zoo». | It is an abbreviation of the Zoological Gardens of London | It derives from the cry of a lion | It derives from the name of the first keeper of the menagerie | It is a Latin term denoting an enclosure for captive beasts
exam: Identify the statute under which zoological institutions in the United Kingdom are required to hold a licence. | The Zoo Licensing Act of 1981 | The Wildlife Freedom Act of 1828 | The Animal Exhibition Act of 1752 | No such statute is in force
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the contribution of the Bronx Zoo to the conservation of the American bison? | In 1907 it dispatched bison to the Great Plains | In 1907 it acquired the last surviving bison for exhibition | It cloned the American bison from cryopreserved cells | It relocated the American bison to London
exam: Ascertain which zoological institution receives the greatest number of visitors in the United Kingdom. | Chester Zoo | Whipsnade Zoo | Edinburgh Zoo | The menagerie of the Tower of London
